      <section class="signal-section" aria-labelledby="signals-title">
        <div class="wide-section">
          <p class="section-kicker">The reliable second-round signals</p>
          <h2 id="signals-title">The soft and hard sides grew together</h2>
          <p class="section-dek">Change in average Google Trends interest during the latest 52 weeks versus the preceding 52 weeks. Use the filters to isolate the party, lifestyle, and tourism-control terms.</p>
          <div class="filters" aria-label="Filter trend signals">
            <button class="filter-button" type="button" data-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All signals</button>
            <button class="filter-button" type="button" data-filter="party" aria-pressed="false">Party and alcohol</button>
            <button class="filter-button" type="button" data-filter="soft" aria-pressed="false">Coffee and wellness</button>
            <button class="filter-button" type="button" data-filter="control" aria-pressed="false">Tourism control</button>
          </div>
          <div class="signal-chart">
            {signals.map((signal) => (
              <div class:list={['signal-row', signal.value < 0 && 'negative']} data-group={signal.group}>
                <span class="signal-name">{signal.label}</span>
                <span class="bar-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="bar" style={`--value:${Math.max(signal.value, 2)}`}></span></span>
                <span class="signal-value">{signal.value > 0 ? '+' : ''}{signal.value}%<small>{signal.qualifier}</small></span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="axis-note">Google Trends uses a normalized 0 to 100 index, not raw search counts. Percent changes are calculated within each term’s own five-year series. Large figures from lower bases deserve less precision than established, weekly terms.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
        <div class="wide-section method-grid">
          <div class="method-copy">
            <p class="section-kicker">Method and receipts</p>
            <h2 id="method-title">How the thesis was tested</h2>
            <p>The first round used 25 broad terms. The second used 100 new terms in 20 five-term comparisons. Every comparison covered U.S. Google searches over the previous five years, with extra attention to summer 2026.</p>
            <p>The second-round stability screen required regular nonzero observations, adequate relative volume, and a usable prior-year baseline. Twenty-three terms passed. The other 77 remain useful as a map of emerging language, but their percentage changes are too fragile to carry the argument.</p>
            <p>Google Trends measures search attention, not attendance, revenue, drinks consumed, or intent. Venue calendars and visitor surveys provide separate checks on the interpretation.</p>
          </div>
